A||||7||Taijiguy||3.8 into GT?
Z||000000||Taijiguy||08-04-99||08:33 PM||||Got an 80's 3.8 V-6 sitting on a dolly in my garage. Been wondering what hoops I have to jump through, or how many witchdoctors I need to visit to see it in the back of my '85 GT...anyone familiar with this swap? How do the bolt patterns match, are adapters available, etc etc etc......
Z||000001||batboy||08-04-99||09:10 PM||||This swap has been discussed in a number of different posts here on the Forum. The 3800 V6 is a good engine, but it will not bolt up to the Fiero tranny. The easiest way to do the swap is by purchasing a conversion kit offered by several companies. Cost is going to be a least $2000 or more. The below URL might get you started on gathering info:<p>http://www.americancustomeng.com/3800-V6.htm
Z||000002||PaUL||08-05-99||04:54 AM||||The 3.8s from FWD cars will fit the Fiero trans but I dont think they have a flywheel for the manual trans.
Z||000003||batboy||08-05-99||05:38 PM||||Oops! Sorry! PaUL got me! I did some double checking, he's right and I'm wrong. The 3800 engine does bolt up directly to the Fiero transmissions. The two conversions that I'd seen had used the 3800 tranny in the swap, so I mistakenly assumed that they didn't match up. There still is a lot of work involved like custom motor mounts, custom flywheel, etc. Here is another URL about the 3800 swap (it has some links to other people's sites that have done it too.<p>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/index.htm
Z||000004||PaUL||08-05-99||06:31 PM||||I believe all FWD engines produced by GM from 1980 on have the same trans bolt pattern with the exception of Saturn, (Ive never seen the engine on one). This includes all versions of the 3.8, 3.8 supercharged,  the 24 valve 3.4 DOHC, Northstar, Aurora, and a bunch of other forgetable 4 cylinders. 
Z||000005||Taijiguy||08-05-99||06:45 PM||||Thanks guys, <br>This is an older motor, it's out of a Skylark or something. FWD for sure...might even be an odd-fire from the looks of the distributor cap. Of course, I'd make all the necessary mods to change that. I just built the crap out of the 6 that was in it last year..(I'll post those in General discussion later) But I keep eyeballin that 3.8, cause it's easy to get 300 horses out of 'em, and they don't weigh that much more. Anyway, I'm very atentive, so keep the advice comin', please!
Z||000006||PaUL||08-05-99||07:30 PM||||Sounds like a plan Taijiguy. West Coast Fieros sells exhaust headers for the 3.8 conversion as well as the mounts. I think you can use the flywheel from a 3.8 F car. You can keep the costs down by keeping it a carbed engine. Theres tons of parts for the 3.8. If yours is an odd fire, you can change it to even fire with a split pin crank and new distributor. 
Z||000007||terryk||08-05-99||11:51 PM||||For the automatic, it should bolt right to the 125C.<p>For the manual, you might want to checking the flywheel from a 3L N car. That might work since it came with the Getrag too. Should work for the 4 spd also.<p>The bolt pattern is not the issue. It's the flywheel on the manual. The flexplate for the auto should be fine.
